<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ganesha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ganapati, Vinayaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ganesha is an elephant-headed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and Parvati, worshipped as the remover of obstacles and the patron of beginnings, learning and the arts. He is invoked at the start of rituals, journeys and undertakings, and is honoured across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and in Buddhist and Jain traditions. His annual festival, Ganesh Chaturthi, is especially popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganesha.jpg" alt="Ganesha" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganesha_Basohli_miniature_circa_1730_Dubost_p73.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ganesha is mentioned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> texts between the 1st century BCE and the 2nd century CE, and a few images from the 4th and 5th centuries CE have been documented. An elephant-headed figure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> coins of the 1st century BCE has been proposed as an early depiction, but this is strongly contested. The first terracotta images date from the 1st century CE, and the earliest stone icons were carved at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> in Kushan times. The title gaṇapati appears twice in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rig Veda</a>, but there it refers to Brahmanaspati and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, not to Ganesha. The detailed stories of his birth and elephant head come from the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, composed from about 600 onwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ganesha is identified by his elephant head and four arms. He is also called Ganapati, Vinayaka and Pillaiyar. Hindu texts name him as the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and Parvati, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> give different accounts of his birth. In the most recurrent story Parvati made him from clay to guard her, and Shiva beheaded him when he came between them and replaced the head with an elephant's.</p><p>Devotees honour him as Vighneshvara, the Lord of Obstacles, held both to place obstacles in the way of those who need to be checked and to remove them for those who begin something new. He is also the patron of letters and learning. In the Ganapatya tradition he is the Supreme Being, and the Ganesha Purana and Mudgala Purana are its principal texts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> of all denominations invoke Ganesha at the beginning of prayers and ceremonies, and dancers and musicians, particularly in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, begin performances with a prayer to him. He is placed at the doorway of many temples, and the Ashtavinayak, eight shrines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, are dedicated to him. The ten-day festival of Ganesh Chaturthi begins with the bringing in of clay idols, which are immersed in water at its end. In 1893 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Lokmanya Tilak</a> turned it from private family celebrations into a public event, to build unity against British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>.</p><p>From about the 10th century he became the principal deity of traders. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> he appears as the god Vināyaka, often dancing. Some Jains worship him, and the earliest known Jain statue dates to about the 9th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ganesha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibrahim Adil Shah II, who praised the goddess Saraswati and the god <b>Ganesha</b> in his songs, was remembered for his openness towards Hindu subjects, musicians and scholars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 3rd century, as Buddhism spread under the Northern Wei, strong influences from the Indian subcontinent entered the native religion: a cult of <b>Ganesha</b> is attested in 531, and processions of carts and floats bearing gods’ images, with music and chanting, came the same way.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ganesha</b>, the elephant-headed remover of obstacles, is honoured at the start of almost every undertaking.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>A Hindu legend narrates that Vyasa composed it and that <b>Ganesha</b> broke off one of his tusks and used it to write the epic down.</blockquote><a href="/work/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first section says that <b>Ganesha</b> wrote the text down to Vyasa's dictation, but scholars regard this as a later interpolation, and the Critical Edition does not include it.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>The Shiva temple, 47 metres tall and 34 metres wide, has five chambers; the central one holds a three-metre statue of Shiva Mahadeva, and the others hold Agastya, <b>Ganesha</b> and Durga as slayer of the Bull demon.</blockquote><a href="/work/prambanan">Prambanan · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
